on-arrival: {! AnatolianRules.cyprusRoadmap.awardRally(Q); AnatolianRules.cyprusRoadmap.startEndingMusic(Q); !}

= Kıbrıs'ın Dağlarına Yazdık!

There was very little pavement showing in front of İstanbul's Municipal Palace by the afternoon. People from Fatih, across various bridges and neighborhoods where we could barely get enough people into the room during our meetings, came to Saraçhane. Turkish flags were waving everywhere, and blue shirts were packed into the area. Banners were made, and everyone was chanting a name which no longer felt like just one person's.

"Karaoğlan! Karaoğlan!"

For weeks, our country had been in an uproar. Mothers were waiting to hear their sons' names on the evening news, fathers were acting like they didn't care but had their sons' last letters tucked away, and families in Cyprus were sitting behind closed windows, hoping the next person outside would be their savior.

As Bülent Ecevit walked up to the microphone, a huge round of applause broke out. He waved to the crowd of blue-shirted people, but no matter how hard he tried, he couldn't be heard as the crowd cheered "Kıbrıs Fatihi" and "Karaoğlan". An old lady at the front clutched her flag tightly and cried.

Our army had crossed the sea and fought against the junta. After a long time of arguing, threatening and constant requests for more time, our delegation had finally shown the Turkish Cypriots that they weren't alone.

[? if cyprus_final_result == "Güneş Plan" : Not only had we won militarily, but we had ensured our people would have a place in an independent federal Cyprus through the six Turkish cantons. This was achieved without our army having to launch a second operation, as our soldiers had cleared a path for us and Güneş and his delegation had taken us to the end. ?]

[? if cyprus_final_result == "Denktaş Plan" : A Turkish region in the north would give our community the security and self-government for which it had struggled. The battlefield’s gains had been turned into a federal agreement. Our people would no longer have to ask, every morning, whether the promises of the previous evening would still protect them. ?]

[? if cyprus_final_result == "Historical Division" : Our soldiers had secured a northern position that no government in Nicosia could simply wish away. There was still a political settlement to be won, and no one on that platform could honestly pretend otherwise. But the Turkish Cypriots were no longer waiting helplessly for the world to notice them. ?]

[? if cyprus_final_result == "Return to the 1960 Constitutional Order" : The agreement had kept Cyprus united while giving our community new constitutional safeguards. We had not obtained the territorial settlement with which we had entered the negotiations, but we had brought the crisis to a close without another offensive. In the square, the people welcomed the promise that the rights won at Geneva would now be made real. ?]

Ecevit began talking about our soldiers arriving on the beaches, the families on the island and the peace we had come to achieve. He didn't have to shout, as the square was listening intently. When he stopped talking, the chanting would start again.

He then turned to talk to the people who had said for years we couldn't be left-nationalists. Demirel and Türkeş weren't forgotten about. For years, the CHP, and especially the new Democratic Left movement within it, had been accused of not being nationalist, of being traitors to the nation and the ideals our country was founded on. However, all of this was silenced today as the government made it happen...

“We will not take lessons in nationalism from Demirel or Türkeş!”

The square erupted. He put his hand up and waited for the microphones.

[? if cyprus_rally_poppy_lifted_late == 0 : The farmers of Western Anatolia had already seen what those words meant. The poppy ban had been lifted before the crisis, despite the pressure from abroad. Their fields, like our country’s decisions, would not be surrendered merely because someone more powerful demanded it. ?]

[? if cyprus_rally_poppy_lifted_late == 1 : Following Cyprus, our government had also removed the ban on growing poppies. As we had worked with the USA during the Cyprus crisis, they couldn't really do much in the near future apart from frown. The farmers in Western Anatolia could return to their fields. We had fought for peace on the island and ensured we didn't lose our right to choose what our people could grow. ?]

“We have written our nationalism not on street walls, but on the soil of Cyprus, on the seabed of the Aegean, and in the poppy fields of Western Anatolia!”

The speech couldn't continue for a few minutes.

Flags were flying everywhere. People were hugging strangers. One young man jumped onto his friend's shoulders and screamed until he couldn't anymore. The old lady at the front was crying but still clutching her flag. Chanting filled the square.

"Karaoğlan! Karaoğlan!"

We had argued for years that our party hadn't forgotten the country we helped build. However, we didn't need a leaflet today. Families in Cyprus would sleep more safely knowing our soldiers were there. Men who had wondered if they would ever see these streets again would return home. Some wouldn't return. Their place in this celebration would never be filled with applause.

We had to ensure peace was worth keeping.

Once Ecevit had finished speaking, the crowd didn't immediately leave. Songs were still being sung, flags were still flying and people were still hanging around. We knew tomorrow there would be arguments. The coalition, the economy and the unfinished work on Kıbrıs would all be there.

However, today, in Saraçhane, we could all stand a little taller knowing what our country had done.

The rally adds one percentage point to CHP's temporary Cyprus Prestige in National Coalition.

- @finish: Kıbrıs'ın Dağlarına Yazdık...

